Good idea. With 5 slots, a few test runs can always be running against the latest `main`, as a continuous error hunt. I'll build a small runner, `dev/soak.py`, that:
- keeps a set number of test runs going, by default 2, so the research agent's 2 slots plus 1 spare stay free;
- packs each new run from a clean export of the current `main`, so new fixes automatically go into the next run;
- when a run ends, records it in the tracker (experiment `soak`), pulls the log, and writes a one-line summary to `experiments/soak.log`: quality, time, which phases failed, and why.

Through a watch on that summary, I'll see every finished run and can react to recurring errors.

The mail agent is finished and merged (`d15f2e7`): locally 8 of 9 mail record types in 401 s instead of 7 of 9 in 515 s, the approval after 106 s instead of 330, and hung tabs are replaced automatically.

**New top risk:** in two sandbox runs the tender portal never opened at all, so no bid was possible and the run scored 0. The analysis agent now examines exactly that first: overloaded sites or our start-up code, and whether a different start strategy would help (longer first load, retries over minutes, starting the bid site first).

**Running now:**
- 3 soak runs of `main` (`de33a85`, so without the mail merge yet; their first results are due any moment);
- the ERP agent (rebase plus a test run);
- the analysis agent.

